  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">DECREASING HUMAN-ELEPHANT CONFLICT IN GABON</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The purpose of this project is to support an elephant conservation effort in Gabon by reducing human-elephant conflict (HEC) and improving local livelihoods. Gabon is the primary remaining stronghold for the species, with an estimated 95,110 elephants (over half the remaining forest elephant population). This project is intended to conserve African forest elephants and their habitat by addressing the impacts of HEC, building government capacity to monitor and respond to HEC incidents, and promoting pro conservation agriculture practices. Specific activities include: (1) piloting a national HEC data collection strategy, including support for equipment, training of government officials, and analysis of collected data; (2) systematically testing three lower specification elephant-proof fence designs, including support for materials and construction and maintenance and monitoring; (3) implementing protocols to gather data on social interactions and behaviors between elephants and local communities, including deploying baseline social surveys and camera traps; (4) assessing and piloting agricultural improvement practices; and (5) updating and publishing a comprehensive national HEC strategy, endorsed by the government of Gabon.</narrative>
  </description>
